.TH VALLUM-QUICK 8 "2026-10-19" "vallum 0.1.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
vallum-quick \- bring a Vallum config up with its routes, DNS and hooks
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vallum-quick
.B up
.I FILE
.RB [ --dry-run ]
.br
.B vallum-quick
.B down
.I FILE
.RB [ --dry-run ]
.br
.B vallum-quick
.B strip
.I FILE
.br
.B vallum-quick
.B attach
.I FILE
.RB [ --dry-run ]
.br
.B vallum-quick
.B detach
.I FILE
.RB [ --dry-run ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B vallum-quick
is the admin-side helper. It starts
.BR vallum (8),
adds routes for each peer's allowed IPs, installs DNS through
.BR resolvconf (8)
when available, and runs the operator's hooks. The daemon never runs any of
this.
.PP
.B attach
and
.B detach
do the same host-level work for an interface a supervisor already owns. The
systemd deployment uses them: \fBvallum@.service\fR runs the daemon and
\fBvallum-quick@.service\fR runs \fBattach\fR once the interface exists, so the
daemon's sandbox never has to allow writes to
.I /etc/resolv.conf
or a shell. A prefix the interface's own address already covers is skipped, so
\fBattach\fR is a no-op on a server and adds the client's default route on a
client.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --dry-run
Print the commands instead of running them.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The
.B [quick]
table holds
.BR dns ,
.BR table
.RB ( auto ", " off " or a number), and "
.BR pre_up ", " post_up ", " pre_down ", " post_down .
Hooks run through
.BR sh (1)
and are the operator's responsibility.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vallum (8),
.BR vallum.toml (5)
